---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import FullBleedHero from '../components/FullBleedHero.astro';
import BentoGrid from '../components/BentoGrid.astro';
import IndiaMap from '../components/IndiaMap.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const is = SITE.impactStats;

const stories = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Story 01 · Farmer income',
    title: 'A second income, not a side hustle.',
    body: [
      'A one-acre paddy plot in Vizianagaram yields, on average, two paddy crops per year and roughly 4 tonnes of dry paddy straw as a co-product. That straw, today, has three usual fates: burnt in the field (most common), used as cattle bedding (a fraction), or left to rot. None of them pay.',
      'Under our model, every tonne of paddy straw we buy goes onto a supply slip, into the digester, and 14 days later, into the farmer\'s bank account. The economics aren\'t transformational — but they convert a free residue into a small, reliable, repeatable income stream that compounds across a 50 km catchment.',
      'For Napier grass — a perennial tropical grass that grows on land otherwise considered uncultivable — we offer contract farming. Suresh\'s family has owned land in this district for three generations. We know which plots have been lying fallow because they\'re stony or low-water. That land grows Napier just fine, and Napier feeds our digester just as well as paddy straw.'
    ],
    ref: 'Reference: NITI Aayog (2023) on paddy-straw availability and disposal economics; SATAT scheme document on CBG feedstock contracts.',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Story 02 · Air',
    title: "The straw doesn't have to burn.",
    body: [
      'Every November and December, the Indo-Gangetic Plain — Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar — turns into a chimney for roughly six weeks. Paddy-stubble burning contributes 30–40% of Delhi\'s PM2.5 during peak smog episodes. The story is not new and the policy responses are not working.',
      'Coastal Andhra Pradesh has the same residue, on a smaller scale, with the same default outcome: burn it because there is no buyer. CBG plants are a buyer. Our plant alone will pull roughly 26,000 tonnes of paddy straw out of the burn cycle every year. That is a small fraction of the national problem and 100% of the local one in our 50 km radius.',
      'We are not pretending one plant fixes Delhi. We are pretending one plant fixes Vizianagaram\'s burning-season air. That is a smaller, more honest claim — and one we can be held to.'
    ],
    ref: 'Reference: CPCB annual PM2.5 reports; IIT-Kanpur Delhi air-quality source apportionment; IARI on stubble-burning economics.',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'Story 03 · Soil',
    title: 'Carbon back into the soil.',
    body: [
      'Indian agricultural soils have lost roughly half of their organic carbon stock since the Green Revolution. Chemical fertilizers replaced organic matter; chemical fertilizers do not feed the soil — they bypass it. The result, three decades in, is a measurable drop in soil-organic-carbon, water-holding capacity, and microbial activity across the very fields that fed the country.',
      'A bio-CNG plant doesn\'t only produce gas. The digestate — the slurry that comes out of the digester after methane is extracted — is one of the densest organic-manure inputs you can put back into a field. We will produce roughly 1 tonne per hour of solid Fermented Organic Manure and 52 m³/day of liquid digestate. All of it is returned to the catchment we sourced from. None of it is sold to a distant fertilizer brand.',
      'That is the real loop: feedstock from farmers, gas to IOCL, manure back to the same farmers. The plant is a translator between a residue economy and a soil economy.'
    ],
    ref: 'Reference: ICAR-IISS Bhopal on soil organic carbon trends; NITI Aayog (2022) on organic manure deficit; MNRE National Bioenergy Programme on FOM as a co-product.',
  },
];
---
<Base title="Impact" description="Three concrete stories on what one small CBG plant actually changes — for farmer income, for air, and for soil.">
  <FullBleedHero
    banner="impact_fom_soil"
    alt="Overhead macro of a hand spreading Fermented Organic Manure on freshly tilled red-brown Andhra soil."
    eyebrow="Impact"
  >
    <h1 class="display text-[color:var(--color-paper)] mt-3 max-w-4xl">
      A 4 TPD plant is small.<br/>
      <span style="color:#FFB300;">Our slice of Andhra gets measurably better.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg max-w-2xl text-[color:var(--color-paper)]/90 leading-relaxed">
      None of these numbers fix India. All of them make a 50 km radius around Thatipadu a better place.
    </p>
  </FullBleedHero>

  <!-- BENTO IMPACT STATS -->
  <Section eyebrow="What changes" title="What one plant actually moves.">
    <BentoGrid hero={is[0]} supporting={[is[1], is[2], is[3]]} />
  </Section>

  <!-- INDIA MAP -->
  <Section eyebrow="The geography of the problem" title="Why this matters where it matters." bg="paper-2">
    <div class="grid-12 items-center">
      <div class="col-8"><IndiaMap /></div>
      <aside class="col-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <p>India's paddy belt and India's smog corridor overlap. Both rest, geographically, on the same set of states that grow most of the country's rice — Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, coastal Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Odisha.</p>
        <p class="mt-3">Our plant sits inside that paddy belt, in coastal Andhra Pradesh. Same problem (paddy straw with no buyer), at a smaller scale than Punjab, with the same default outcome (burning). Same solution.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- THREE STORIES -->
  {stories.map((st, i) => (
    <Section eyebrow={st.eyebrow} title={st.title} numeral={`0${i + 1}`} narrow bg={i % 2 === 1 ? 'paper-2' : 'paper'}>
      <div class="prose-svge drop-cap">
        {st.body.map(p => <p>{p}</p>)}
        <p class="text-sm font-mono text-[color:var(--color-slate-2)] mt-6">{st.ref}</p>
      </div>
    </Section>
  ))}

  <!-- ALIGNED WITH -->
  <Section eyebrow="Aligned with" title="Three national missions." bg="paper-2">
    <div class="grid-12">
      <article class="col-4 border-t-2 border-[color:var(--color-ink)] pt-5">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2">MoPNG</p>
        <h3 class="text-2xl mb-2">SATAT</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-slate)]">Sustainable Alternative Towards Affordable Transportation — 5,000 CBG plants, 15 MMTPA. We have an IOCL Letter of Intent under this scheme.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="col-4 border-t-2 border-[color:var(--color-ink)] pt-5">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Jal Shakti / MNRE</p>
        <h3 class="text-2xl mb-2">GOBARdhan</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-slate)]">Galvanizing Organic Bio-Agro Resources Dhan — ₹10,000 Cr scheme targeting 500 bio-CNG plants from organic waste.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="col-4 border-t-2 border-[color:var(--color-ink)] pt-5">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-2">MNRE</p>
        <h3 class="text-2xl mb-2">National Bioenergy Programme</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-slate)]">Phase-I Central Financial Assistance for biogas plants. Extended to 31 March 2026.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
